From 502fe4febfa8a6bfb99c239316cddb074fabd656 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gtguerreiro-max <295622337+gtguerreiro-max@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 19:24:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] kernel: guest events, semaphores and critical sections wait
 in user space

Under Proton every operation on a host event or semaphore is an ntsync
system call, and a thread whose object is not signaled yet sleeps at once
to be woken a few microseconds later. On top of that every guest wait
wrote the thread's CPU time with GetThreadTimes, a wineserver round trip
that blocks the thread. On the Steam Deck Iron Brigade's main thread made
23,500 voluntary context switches a second and its three job workers about
7,100 each, almost all of them on waits that were satisfied at once.

With runtime.fast_waits (on by default):

- An event or a semaphore keeps its state in an atomic word (FastState,
  kernel/object/fast_sync.h). A set or a release with no queued thread
  only changes the word; with threads queued it hands the signal to them
  first, oldest first, one each for an auto reset event or a semaphore
  count, all of them for a manual reset event. A set followed at once by a
  reset still releases the queued threads, and a wait that starts later
  cannot take a count meant for one. Semaphore limits and return values
  are unchanged.
- A wait takes the word if it can, spins runtime.wait_spin_us (6 us, TSC,
  pause), then queues itself and sleeps on its own thread's park word
  (WaitOnAddress: a futex under Wine, no syscall for a wake without a
  sleeper). Stop, suspend and APC requests set the park word and wake it,
  as their host callback wakes a host wait; the callback then runs at once.
- Waits only the host can do (several objects, alertable, signal-and-wait)
  move the object to its host primitive for good, with its state; threads
  queued on it wait again there. The log names each object that moves.
- A guest re-initialization of an NtCreate* body leaves the queued threads
  asleep out of reach, as the console's new empty wait list does (and as
  the replaced host primitive did).
- RtlEnterCriticalSection spins for the same time by the clock with pause,
  whatever the title's spin count, before it counts itself in the lock and
  waits on the section's event, now such an event.
- ResolveObject finds an existing object under the table's shared lock
  alone, without the identity lock every Ke call queued on.
- WriteKernelTime refreshes X_KTHREAD::kernel_time at most every 4 ms.

runtime.fast_waits=false keeps every old path. runtime.wait_stats logs per
thread, every 5 s, the waits by kind (taken at once, after the spin, after
a sleep, on a host primitive) and the signals.

Steam Deck LCD, GE-Proton11-12 (ntsync), same scenes, runtime.fast_waits
false -> true in the same build (game CPU in % of one core):
- Iron Brigade, FPS Boost 60 fps, MSAA (2 runs each): main thread 24,000 ->
  1,900 voluntary context switches/s, its 3 job workers 7,300 -> 300 each;
  game 266 -> 210 %, wineserver 27 -> 8 %, whole system 330 -> 271 %;
  APU 14.9 -> 8.2 W, CPU cores 3,350 -> 2,400 MHz; 59.4 -> 60.0 fps.
- Iron Brigade 30 fps (no FPS Boost), Deck defaults: game 208 -> 168 %,
  wineserver 22 -> 11 %, APU 6.06 -> 5.71 W.
- Forza Horizon 30 fps, driving, Deck defaults (2 runs each): game 236 ->
  206 %, wineserver 13 -> 7 %, APU 6.54 -> 6.06 W.
- Overlord II 30 fps, first level, Deck defaults: game 115 -> 94 %,
  wineserver 10 -> 6 %, APU 6.13 -> 5.73 W.

Unit tests: tests/unit/kernel/fast_wait_test.cpp (hand-off before a new
wait, set+reset, pulse, a multi-object wait taking over a queued event,
suspend, stop, timeout, and stress of semaphores, events and a critical
section).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
