@@ -54,6 +55,17 @@ Every console synchronization object starts with an `X_DISPATCH_HEADER`, a small
 
 The console keeps a guest block for each object, an `X_OBJECT_HEADER` followed by the object body. `AllocObjectBlock` allocates this block from the system heap or from a workspace. XAM enumerator objects take their blocks from the [title workspace](#title-workspace). `NtCreateEvent`, `NtCreateSemaphore`, `NtCreateMutant` and `NtCreateTimer` allocate this block, so `ObReferenceObjectByHandle` returns the body address for their handles. Every dispatcher body starts with an empty wait list, as the console writes it.
 
+### Waits in User Space
+
+Under Proton every operation on a host event or semaphore is a system call, and a thread whose object is not signaled yet sleeps at once. With `runtime.fast_waits` (on by default) an event or a semaphore keeps its state in an atomic word instead (`object/fast_sync.h`):
+
+- A set or a release with no thread queued only changes the word. With threads queued it hands the signal to them first, oldest first: an auto reset event or a semaphore count to one thread each, a manual reset event to all of them, so a set followed at once by a reset still releases the queued threads, and a wait that starts later cannot take a count meant for one.
+- A wait takes the word if it can, spins for `runtime.wait_spin_us` (6 by default, by the TSC, with `pause`), then queues itself and sleeps on its own thread's word (`WaitOnAddress`, a futex under Wine). A stop, a suspend or an APC request wakes it, as a host callback wakes a host wait.
+- A wait the host must do, on several objects, alertable, or `NtSignalAndWaitForSingleObjectEx`, moves the object to its host primitive for good, with its state, and the threads queued on it go on waiting there. The log names each object that moves.
+- `RtlEnterCriticalSection` spins for the same time, whatever the title's spin count, before it counts itself in the lock and waits on the section's event, which is such an event.
+
+`KeWaitForSingleObject` and the other waits refresh the thread's `kernel_time` at most every 4 ms then, because `GetThreadTimes` is a wineserver round trip under Wine. With `runtime.wait_stats` the runtime logs every 5 s, per thread, its waits by kind (taken at once, after the spin, after a sleep, or on a host primitive) and its signals.
+
 ## Threads
 
 Each guest thread is a host thread. A `KernelThread`, from `rex/kernel/object/kernel_thread.h`, holds the host thread, the guest register state, the guest `X_KTHREAD` block, the guest stack and a PCR, the processor control region that guest code reads through register r13. A `KernelThread` built with a host function instead of a guest entry point is a host worker of the runtime, such as `Kernel Dispatch`.
